  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule are both in place, and the rule is enabled. It has already run once and reported status `ok`, meaning it ran without errors and hasn't fired.\n\n**1. What the data holds:** about 14,000 web requests between Oct 4 and Dec 3, 2026, with fields for response code, bytes, client IP, URL, file extension, host, OS and location. Only three response codes appear: 200 (12,832), 404 (801) and 503 (441). The 503 responses all have 0 bytes. Every request has the same source country (US), so I didn't chart source country.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-68ac4dfc Web logs overview\"** ([open](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-68ac4dfc-web-logs-overview-cfec162b7c61)) has seven panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Share of each response code\n- Top 10 destination countries\n- Top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes\n\nIts default time range is fixed to Oct 4 \u2013 Dec 4, 2026, the span of the data. I only confirmed the dashboard and its panels exist; I didn't open them in the browser to check that they display correctly.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-68ac4dfc 5xx spike\"** (id `bcb2638f-f384-4ee6-8332-19131187320e`) counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\". It checks every minute and fires when there are more than 10 in the last 5 minutes. It has no notification actions, so when it fires the alert appears only in Kibana's alerts view. You'd need to add a connector, such as Slack or email, to get notified.",
